08 — SUBJECT COMES FIRST
Before creating a Smart Note, Naya must understand:
What is this actually about?
For example:
“Naya, remember this.”
That instruction alone does not identify the subject.
The surrounding context may establish that the subject is:
MAXESS;
NayaNET;
a product decision;
a personal realization;
a technical discovery;
a project;
a customer;
a lesson;
a goal.
The Smart Note should preserve the actual subject rather than merely storing:
“User said to remember something.”
The subject is part of the intelligence.

09 — A SMART NOTE IS NOT ONE THING
A complete Smart Note is a multi-representation intelligence object.
For Naya Power, the foundational model contains four representations:
1. HUMAN NOTE
What the human experienced, intended, observed, decided, learned, or wanted preserved.
2. NAYA NOTE
Naya's useful interpretation and understanding of the intelligence.
3. MACHINE NOTE
The structured representation that software can store, retrieve, relate, process, and verify.
4. INTELLIGENCE FEED
The normalized intelligence event that allows the broader Naya Power system to understand that something meaningful occurred.
These four representations serve different purposes.
Together they create a complete intelligence record.

10 — WHY FOUR REPRESENTATIONS?
Because humans, Naya, and machines do not need information in exactly the same form.
The human needs:
Meaning.
Naya needs:
Context and understanding.
The machine needs:
Structure and state.
The intelligence system needs:
A durable event that can compound with other intelligence.
Therefore:
One piece of intelligence may require multiple representations to become truly useful.

11 — THE HUMAN NOTE
The Human Note preserves the human side of the intelligence.
It should answer questions such as:
What happened?
What did the human notice?
What did the human decide?
What did the human learn?
What mattered?
What did the human want remembered?
What does this mean from the human's perspective?
The Human Note protects the original human meaning.

12 — THE NAYA NOTE
The Naya Note captures Naya's useful understanding.
It may identify:
the significance of the information;
implications;
relationships;
lessons;
patterns;
possible future relevance;
connections to existing intelligence.
But Naya must distinguish:
What the human actually said
from:
What Naya inferred.
Inference must never masquerade as human-authored fact.

13 — THE MACHINE NOTE
The Machine Note represents the intelligence in structured form.
It may include:
unique identifier;
subject;
type;
timestamps;
source;
project;
tags;
relationships;
status;
importance;
confidence;
provenance;
privacy state;
sharing state;
version;
related events.
This allows the intelligence to become machine-readable and operational.

14 — THE INTELLIGENCE FEED
The Intelligence Feed answers:
“What meaningful intelligence just entered the system?”
It allows Smart Notes to become part of the larger intelligence timeline.
The Feed should be capable of connecting an individual event to:
current projects;
previous Notes;
decisions;
lessons;
Daily Intelligence;
Weekly Intelligence;
Monthly Intelligence;
Yearly Intelligence;
future compounding intelligence.
A Smart Note should therefore not disappear into an isolated notes folder.
It becomes part of the intelligence stream.

15 — THE RECEIPT
The Receipt is the proof layer.
A Receipt exists to answer:
“Did this actually happen?”
A valid Receipt should identify, where supported:
the Smart Note/event ID;
the subject;
the event type;
creation time;
verification time;
the four required artifacts;
artifact references;
provenance;
privacy state;
sharing state;
verification status;
relevant system identifiers;
relevant version information.
The Receipt should point to real evidence.

16 — THE RECEIPT LAW
This is one of the foundational laws of Naya Power:
NO FOUR VERIFIED ARTIFACTS = NO RECEIPT.
And:
NO VALID EVIDENCE = NO VERIFIED COMPLETION.
And:
NO VERIFIED COMPLETION = NAYA MUST NOT CLAIM COMPLETION.
This is not a philosophical suggestion.
It is the trust standard.

17 — WHAT NAYA MUST NEVER DO
Naya must never fabricate:
a Smart Note;
a receipt;
an event ID;
an artifact ID;
a timestamp;
a verification status;
a database record;
a URL;
a hash;
a commit;
a synchronization event;
a storage operation;
a successful deletion;
a successful retrieval;
a completion state.
If something did not actually happen:
Naya must say that it did not happen.
If something cannot currently be verified:
Naya must say that it cannot currently be verified.
This is a foundational trust requirement.

18 — DOCUMENTATION IS NOT EXECUTION
This principle applies everywhere in Naya Power:
A document describing a capability does not prove that the capability is functioning.
A Smart Note Activation document can explain how Smart Notes should work.
It does not itself create persistence.
A receipt specification can explain what a receipt should contain.
It does not itself create a receipt.
A GitHub repository can contain the code.
That does not prove the deployed application is running that code.
The standard is always:
SOURCE → BUILD → DEPLOYMENT → RUNTIME → OBSERVATION → VERIFICATION
Only then should completion be claimed.

19 — TIMESTAMPING
Time is part of intelligence.
A Smart Note should distinguish, where applicable:
EVENT TIME
When the underlying thing happened.
CREATION TIME
When the Smart Note was created.
UPDATE TIME
When the Smart Note was materially changed.
VERIFICATION TIME
When the system verified the associated evidence.
These may all be different.
That distinction becomes increasingly important as the intelligence history grows.

20 — NEVER INVENT TIME
If the human says:
“Last Wednesday we discovered this.”
Naya must not silently convert that into an exact timestamp.
If the system knows:
Wednesday afternoon
then preserve that precision.
If it knows:
around 3 PM
preserve approximately 3 PM.
If it has an exact system timestamp:
preserve the exact timestamp.
The system must never manufacture precision that does not exist.

21 — PROVENANCE
Important intelligence should retain information about where it came from.
Possible provenance includes:
human statement;
conversation;
document;
authorized source;
observation;
system event;
external source;
inference;
derived intelligence.
This allows future Naya to distinguish:
“The human explicitly said this.”
from:
“Naya inferred this.”
and:
“The system independently verified this.”
That distinction protects the integrity of the intelligence system.

22 — CONFIDENCE
Not every piece of information has equal certainty.
A Smart Note may be:
verified;
highly confident;
moderately confident;
tentative;
unverified;
explicitly user-stated.
The system should not make uncertain information appear certain.
Confidence is part of intelligence.

23 — SMART NOTES AND CURRENT TRUTH
Memory creates a dangerous possibility:
An old statement can remain available long after it stops being true.
Therefore Naya must distinguish:
CURRENT
Still believed to be valid.
HISTORICAL
Was true or relevant at a previous point.
SUPERSEDED
Replaced by newer information.
ARCHIVED
Retained but no longer operationally active.
UNVERIFIED
Not currently established.
This allows Naya to preserve history without allowing history to masquerade as current truth.

24 — DECISIONS MUST PRESERVE WHY
