43 — WHAT THE HUMAN EXPERIENCES
The human experience should remain simple.
The person should not need to understand:
database schemas;
event tables;
artifact storage;
hashes;
APIs;
synchronization queues;
internal identifiers.
They should simply be able to say:
“Naya, make a note of this.”
And, when the system is functioning properly, Naya handles the complexity.
The complexity belongs in the system.
Not in the human's head.

44 — WHAT THE HUMAN SHOULD SEE
When a Smart Note is successfully created, the experience should ultimately provide a meaningful confirmation.
For example:
Smart Note captured.
Subject: MAXESS Assessment Onboarding
Intelligence: Clear outcome explanation reduces onboarding confusion.
Status: VERIFIED
Receipt: Available
And the human should be able to inspect the underlying evidence if desired.

45 — THE RECEIPT SHOULD NOT BE NOISE
A receipt is not intended to overwhelm the human with technical information.
Its purpose is trust.
The human should be able to see the simple answer:
Yes. It was captured.
And, if desired:
Here is the evidence.
This creates a layered experience:
SIMPLE
What happened?
DETAILED
What was captured?
TECHNICAL
Show me the underlying evidence.
The system can serve all three levels without forcing everyone into the technical layer.

46 — WHEN THE SYSTEM CANNOT COMPLETE THE CAPTURE
A trustworthy Naya does not pretend.
If persistence is unavailable:
“I can identify and prepare the Smart Note, but I cannot verify that it has been persisted right now.”
If one required artifact fails:
“The Smart Note capture is incomplete. I do not have a valid receipt yet.”
If synchronization with the Hub fails:
“The intelligence was captured locally, but Hub synchronization has not yet been verified.”
The correct behavior is:
Tell the truth. Preserve what can safely be preserved. Continue when the system allows.

47 — HUB UNAVAILABILITY DOES NOT ERASE INTELLIGENCE
The Intelligent Hub may temporarily be unavailable.
That should not automatically mean the underlying intelligence disappears.
Where the architecture supports durable local or source-side persistence, the system should preserve the event and synchronize when the Hub becomes available.
The system should distinguish:
CAPTURED
The intelligence exists.
SYNCED
The Hub received it.
VERIFIED
The required evidence has been validated.
These are different states.

48 — NO SILENT FAILURE
If something fails, the system should not quietly behave as though it succeeded.
The human should not discover days later that:
“Actually, none of those Notes were saved.”
Failure should be visible.
The system should preserve enough information to diagnose:
what was attempted;
what succeeded;
what failed;
what remains pending;
what should happen next.
That principle connects directly to Continuous Action + No Dead Ends.

49 — SMART NOTE IDEMPOTENCY
If the same request is accidentally submitted twice, the system should avoid unnecessary duplicate intelligence where technically possible.
The goal is:
One meaningful event should not accidentally become ten identical memories.
However, legitimate updates should remain distinguishable from duplicates.
For example:
ORIGINAL
“The assessment opening needs improvement.”
UPDATE
“We tested a new opening and completion improved.”
Those should not be collapsed merely because they concern the same subject.

50 — SMART NOTES SHOULD BE CONNECTED
A Smart Note becomes more valuable when connected to related intelligence.
For example:
PROBLEM
Users are confused.
↓
EXPERIMENT
New explanation tested.
↓
RESULT
Completion improved.
↓
LESSON
Explain the outcome before asking for action.
↓
STANDARD
